From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sinful1992 <sinful1992@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 10:04:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Wake a sleeping library disk when a video's item is fetched

Whole-file stream starts on this server sometimes wait 9-13 s for the
first byte. Over 2640 starts (11-04..11-17) the 21 slow ones are a fixed
time per storage volume (9.1 s on one NAS volume, 13.3 s on the other),
on every client, and come almost only after an hour or more with no
reads on that volume: 15 of 81 starts after >60 min idle, 0 of 306 after
5-30 min. The StreamTiming line (0014) puts all of it in the file open +
first read. That is a sleeping disk spinning up.

A viewer is on the details page for a few seconds before Play, and that
page fetches the item (GET /Items/{id}, also the legacy
/Users/{userId}/Items/{id}). When the item is a playable video backed by
one local file, read 64 KiB at its start, middle and end in the
background, so the disk spins up then instead of after Play.

It never delays or fails the request: the read runs on the thread pool,
errors are logged at Debug and dropped, each file is read at most once
per 5 minutes, at most 2 reads run at once (more are skipped, not
queued), and the per-file memory is capped at 4096 entries. Folders,
.strm shortcuts, disc folders (BluRay/DVD), remote paths and virtual
items are skipped. Log: "Prewarm <id> <ms> ms, <bytes> bytes, trigger
item" at Information (at most one line per file per 5 minutes).

Tests: MediaPrewarmTests (16), UserLibraryControllerPrewarmTests (2,
fail without the controller hook); both share MediaPrewarm.Shared and
run in one non-parallel collection.
